        "text": "Iran has made comments that could be described as outright crazy over the years.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Ayatollah Khomeini made statements that were part of Iran's pattern of rhetoric.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Leadership figures in Iran have consistently used inflammatory language.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker considers Iran’s past statements to be credible evidence of its current intentions.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Past speech acts by Iranian leaders should be held accountable in present assessments of Iran.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes Iran cannot credibly distance itself from its historical rhetoric.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the credibility of Iran’s future statements as undermined by past behavior.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts the characterization of Iranian rhetoric as 'bombastic,' 'blatant,' 'threatening,' and 'crazy' as accurate and objective.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 64496,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64496.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "You know, absolutely. And, you know, Doug, I think we talked last week. I can't remember if we talked before, after I spoke with Secretary Kerry. But I have an editorial in the Wall Street Journal this morning, in fact, on this topic, and it makes exactly that point. And that is, on what basis do we have that we can trust Iran on anything? You know, we go down a long, long list of them working against American interests, of them killing Americans. Hezbollah, Hamas, and this stretches from North Korea to South America to Mexico. And yet, this is the regime that we feel we can trust on one of the most critical national security issues of our day. And I just think it's foolishness to presume that they're going to comply with an agreement that they don't view as in their national interest.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I have an editorial in the Wall Street Journal today on this topic.",
